<article class="env-lemma" id="00I6">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/00I6">Lemma <span data-tag="00I6">10.47.1</span>.</a> Let $R \to S$ be a ring map. Assume </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li class="custom-item" data-marker="(a)"><p>
$\mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(R)$ is irreducible, 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="(b)"><p>
$R \to S$ is flat, 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="(c)"><p>
$R \to S$ is of finite presentation, 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="(d)"><p>
the fibre rings $S \otimes _ R \kappa (\mathfrak p)$ have irreducible spectra for a dense collection of primes $\mathfrak p$ of $R$. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
  <p> Then $\mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(S)$ is irreducible. This is true more generally with (b) $+$ (c) replaced by “the map $\mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(S) \to \mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(R)$ is open”. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      The assumptions (b) and (c) imply that the map on spectra is open, see Proposition <a href="/tag/00I1" data-tag="00I1">10.41.8</a>. Hence the lemma follows from Topology, Lemma <a href="/tag/004Z" data-tag="004Z">5.8.14</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>